## 3. JavaEE/CDI Transaction Retry

### Bean-Managed Transactions (BMT)

```java
@InterceptorBinding
@Retention(RUNTIME)
@Target({METHOD, TYPE})
public @interface Retryable {}

@Interceptor
@Retryable
public class RetryInterceptor {
    @Inject
    private TransactionService txService;

    @AroundInvoke
    public Object retry(InvocationContext ctx) throws Exception {
        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= 5; attempt++) {
            try {
                return txService.executeInNewTx(() -> ctx.proceed());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                if (isRetryable(e) && attempt < 5) {
                    Thread.sleep((long)(Math.pow(2, attempt) * 200 + Math.random() * 1000));
                    continue;
                }
                throw e;
            }
        }
        throw new RuntimeException("Max retries exceeded");
    }
}

@Stateless
@TransactionManagement(TransactionManagementType.BEAN)
public class TransactionService {
    @Resource
    private UserTransaction utx;

    public Object executeInNewTx(Callable<Object> work) throws Exception {
        utx.begin();
        try {
            Object result = work.call();
            utx.commit();
            return result;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            utx.rollback();
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```

### Container-Managed Transactions (CMT)

```java
// The retry interceptor calls a REQUIRES_NEW method, so the container
// creates a fresh transaction on each attempt.
@Stateless
public class AccountService {
    @Retryable
    @TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.NOT_SUPPORTED)
    public void transfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) {
        doTransfer(from, to, amount);  // interceptor wraps this
    }

    @TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void doTransfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // actual DB operations — container manages the transaction
    }
}
```

