## 4. Set-Based Operations with CTEs

CockroachDB executes CTEs in parallel across nodes. Single-statement CTE transactions get **server-side auto-retry** (implicit transaction) and eliminate all 40001 retries.

**❌ NEVER: Multi-statement explicit transaction for atomic operations**

```sql
-- BAD: 5+ round trips, holds locks across all statements, 40001-prone
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO transaction (id) VALUES (gen_random_uuid()) RETURNING id;
-- app code captures txn_id
SELECT balance FROM account WHERE id = 'acc1' FOR UPDATE;
SELECT balance FROM account WHERE id = 'acc2' FOR UPDATE;
INSERT INTO transaction_item (...) VALUES (...);
INSERT INTO transaction_item (...) VALUES (...);
UPDATE account SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'acc1';
UPDATE account SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 'acc2';
COMMIT;
```

**✅ ALWAYS: Single-statement CTE (one round-trip, auto-retried)**

```sql
-- GOOD: One statement, server-side retry, parallel execution across nodes
-- Benchmark: p99 drops from 4.45s to 0.30s, retries from 0.43 avg to 0.00
WITH input_data(account_id, amount) AS (
    VALUES ('acc1'::UUID, -100.00), ('acc2'::UUID, 100.00)
),
new_tx AS (
    INSERT INTO transaction (id) VALUES (gen_random_uuid()) RETURNING id
),
locked AS (
    SELECT a.id, a.balance FROM account a
    JOIN input_data i ON a.id = i.account_id FOR UPDATE
),
items AS (
    INSERT INTO transaction_item (transaction_id, account_id, amount, running_balance)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM new_tx), i.account_id, i.amount, a.balance + i.amount
    FROM input_data i JOIN locked a ON a.id = i.account_id RETURNING *
)
UPDATE account SET balance = balance + i.amount
FROM input_data i WHERE account.id = i.account_id;
```

**Set-based bulk delete (replaces N individual DELETEs):**

```sql
-- GOOD: Delete 999 rows in a single implicit transaction (~30ms vs 1+ second)
WITH to_delete(id) AS (
    VALUES ('uuid1'::UUID), ('uuid2'::UUID), ... ('uuid999'::UUID)
)
DELETE FROM target_table t USING to_delete d WHERE t.id = d.id;
```

**Refactoring stored procedures to CTEs:**

```sql
-- Pass parameters via CTE, chain operations as CTE steps
WITH vars AS (SELECT 'acc1'::UUID AS from_id, 'acc2'::UUID AS to_id, 100.00 AS amount),
step1 AS (UPDATE account SET balance = balance - (SELECT amount FROM vars) WHERE id = (SELECT from_id FROM vars) RETURNING *),
step2 AS (UPDATE account SET balance = balance + (SELECT amount FROM vars) WHERE id = (SELECT to_id FROM vars) RETURNING *)
SELECT * FROM step1 UNION ALL SELECT * FROM step2;
```

